# Hexagonal Architecture

This project follows hexagonal (ports & adapters), onion, and clean architecture.

## Layer Definitions

```
presentation → application → domain ← infrastructure
```

### Domain Layer (`domain/`)
- Pure TypeScript, zero framework dependencies (no NestJS decorators)
- Contains: entities, value objects, events, errors, repository port interfaces
- Port interfaces define contracts: `{Entity}RepositoryPort`
- DI tokens use `Symbol('NAME')` or `const NAME = Symbol('NAME')`

### Application Layer (`application/`)
- May use `@Injectable()` from NestJS
- Contains: use cases, event handlers, application DTOs
- Use cases inject ports via `@Inject(TOKEN)`, never concrete implementations
- Event handlers use `@OnEvent()` and delegate to use cases

### Infrastructure Layer (`infrastructure/`)
- Implements domain ports using concrete technologies
- Contains: Prisma repositories, external service adapters
- Classes implement port interfaces: `class EntityRepository implements EntityRepositoryPort`

### Presentation Layer (`presentation/`)
- HTTP-facing NestJS controllers with Swagger decorators
- Contains: controllers, REST DTOs with class-validator
- DTOs use `fromDomain()` or `fromApplication()` static factory methods

## Dependency Rules

- Domain imports nothing from other layers
- Application imports from domain only
- Infrastructure imports from domain (implements ports)
- Presentation imports from application (uses use cases and DTOs)
- Infrastructure and presentation never import from each other

```typescript
// CORRECT: use case injects port interface
@Inject(CREDENTIALS_REPOSITORY)
private readonly credentialsRepository: CredentialsRepositoryPort

// WRONG: use case imports concrete repository
import { CredentialsRepository } from '../../infrastructure/repositories/credentials.repository';
```

## Port & Adapter Pattern

### Ports (interfaces in `domain/ports/`)

```typescript
export const ENTITY_REPOSITORY = Symbol('ENTITY_REPOSITORY');

export interface EntityRepositoryPort {
  create(input: CreateEntityInput): Promise<Entity>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<Entity | null>;
}
```

### Adapters (implementations in `infrastructure/`)

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class EntityRepository implements EntityRepositoryPort {
  constructor(private readonly prisma: PrismaService) {}
  // ... implement all port methods
}
```

### Module Wiring

```typescript
@Module({
  providers: [
    { provide: ENTITY_REPOSITORY, useClass: EntityRepository },
    SomeUseCase,
  ],
})
```

## Anti-Patterns

- Domain layer importing NestJS decorators (except for event interfaces from common)
- Use cases instantiating infrastructure classes directly
- Controllers accessing repositories directly (must go through use cases)
- Importing from another feature module (use event bus instead)
- Repository implementations containing business logic
